## Goal

Today keyboard controls are fixed for everyone (`CONFIG.bindings`), and the Settings note says "Keyboard controls stay the same." Add **keyboard key customization per fighter**, matching how controller controls already work:

- Settings › **Controls** gets a new subgroup, **Keyboard controls** ("Commandes du clavier"), placed directly under the existing **Controller controls** subgroup, with a one-line note and a **Customize keyboard controls** button.
- That button opens the same three-layer flow as the controller: the **fighter roster** → the chosen fighter's **keyboard editor** → a **key binding dialog**.
- Each fighter keeps its own saved keyboard layout, stored by fighter id. A fighter the player never customized plays with today's default keys (`CONFIG.bindings`), so nothing changes for anyone who never opens the screen.

## Design rules (do not break these)

- **One character system.** Never special-case a fighter id in shared code. The actions a fighter's editor lists come from its definition, the same way `controllerActions(def)` does for the controller. A fighter added later works with no change to this feature.
- **Codenames stay the contract.** The keyboard still produces exactly the same control codenames (`runLeft`, `runRight`, `mouvementLeft`, `mouvementRight`, `down`, `jump`, `extra_attack`, `shield`, `attack1`…`attack5`, `pause`) and the same snapshot, so the Fighter, the CPU, Mash Combo, Online Play's `encodeFrame` and the lockstep never learn that keys were remapped. Respect `codename_rule` (`mouvement`/`mouvment` spelling).
- **The keyboard has no ground/air resolution.** Unlike the controller, a key press makes no `pressedOnGround` / `pressedInAir` claim. So a keyboard layout binds **controls** (codenames), not `ground:`/`air:` action ids. In the editor, one row per control. For each combat button, show the fighter's real move names for it: ground move, and its air move when it differs (for example "Jab / Floating Straight"), using `moveFor` and the move art the controller editor already uses (`EditorLayer.rowArt` / `previewFrame`). A button the fighter has no move on is not listed, the same as in the controller editor. Its key stays whatever it defaults to and still does nothing for that fighter.
- **Menus are untouchable.** `CONFIG.menuBindings` (arrows / WASD, Enter / Space / J, Esc / Backspace / K) never changes and is never read from a keyboard layout. No layout can lock a player out of menus.
- **Pause is always reachable.** Esc always pauses, whatever the layout. The player may bind extra keys to Pause, and may move P, but Esc is permanent: show it in the Pause row as a fixed, non-removable key. Update `battle-screen.js` and `practice-screen.js` so they ask the input manager whether a code is Pause for the current fighter, instead of reading `CONFIG.bindings.pause` directly.
- **Reserved keys** can never be bound: Esc (Pause only, see above), `Backquote` (the debug overlay, `CONFIG.debug.overlayKey`), Tab, the modifier keys pressed on their own (Shift, Control, Alt, Meta, CapsLock, ContextMenu), and the browser/OS keys (F1–F12, PrintScreen, the OS key). Keep the list in one exported constant and test it.
- **Keys are physical.** Store `KeyboardEvent.code`, never `.key`, so AZERTY and QWERTY players get the same physical layout, exactly like `CONFIG.bindings` already does. For display, use the keyboard's own label: `navigator.keyboard.getLayoutMap()` where it is available (Chromium), else a fallback that extends `keyCapLabel` (`KeyQ` → Q, `Comma` → `,`, `ArrowLeft` → ←, `Space` → Space/Espace, and so on). Move or share that helper rather than duplicating it.
- **Text fields keep their keys.** While Online Play's name, room or code field has focus, gameplay and remapped keys never act (see **Text fields**). Do not regress this.
- **One storage owner.** Only `js/core/settings.js` touches storage.

## Data model: `js/data/keyboard-profiles.js` (new)

Create it next to `controller-profiles.js`, in the same style and with a header comment like its siblings. It owns:

- `KEYBOARD_CONTROLS`: the bindable controls in editor order, each with its category (reuse the controller's categories: Movement = `runLeft`, `runRight`, `down`, `jump`, `mouvementLeft`, `mouvementRight`; Defense = `shield`; Ground/Aerial/Special per the fighter's moves, using `specialAbilities` exactly as the controller editor does; Other = `pause`).
- `keyboardControls(def)`: the controls this fighter can bind (universal ones always, combat buttons only if the fighter has a move on them).
- `DEFAULT_KEYBOARD`: built from `CONFIG.bindings`. It is the same default for every fighter. Don't invent per-fighter keyboard defaults.
- A binding is an ordered array of 1 to `MAX_KEYS_PER_CONTROL` codes (2). A primary key and an optional secondary key, like today's `KeyA` + `ArrowLeft`. The first key is what the ability HUD shows.
- `RESERVED_KEYS`, and `isBindableKey(code)`.
- `resolveKeyboardProfile(def, stored)`: defaults overlaid with the stored layout, control by control. Return a full map `codename → codes[]` with Esc forced into `pause`.
- `keyboardConflicts(profile)`: an **error** when one key is bound to two controls that can both act. Allowed shared keys, not conflicts: none for now. Keep it strict and simple. Also an error when a control is left with no key at all (Pause excluded, since it always has Esc). A **warning** (informational only) when a key is also a menu key the player might not expect in play (for example binding Enter). Note that J and K are menu keys too and that's fine, so don't warn on today's defaults.
- `sanitizeStoredKeyboardProfile(raw)` / `sanitizeStoredKeyboardProfiles(raw)`: drop anything malformed entry by entry (unknown control, non-string code, reserved key, duplicates, too many keys). Never drop the whole profile or the other fighters' profiles because of one bad entry. A stored layout equal to the default is forgotten (stored as nothing).

No fighter id may appear in this module. Add a test that checks that, the way `controller-profiles.test.mjs` does.

